# W-E: the mobile origin holds a phone's pairing seal key in localStorage, so
# the cost of any injected script here is the whole pairing. The policy below is
# strict everywhere it can be without foreclosing a designed capability.
#
# connect-src deliberately does NOT name a single relay: pairing packages carry
# their own relay and outbox URLs, and a self-hosted relay runs the same locked
# contract, so pinning would make that unreachable from this origin. CSP grammar
# cannot express "any host, but only the relay this pairing names"; that
# policing lives where it already is — the app connects only to URLs the pairing
# package carries, which the harness proves behaviourally with a decoy endpoint.
# A deployment wanting a stricter pin ships its own _headers.
#
# wss: is listed explicitly and is not redundant: the phone's live path opens a
# raw WebSocket, and whether an https: scheme-source also matches wss: is
# engine-dependent under CSP3 — our gate runs Chromium while the field runs iOS
# Safari, so omitting it could pass here and kill the phone's transport there.
/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https: wss:; img-src 'self' data:; worker-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'none'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=(), usb=()

/
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/index.html
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/service-worker.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/mobile-app.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate
